.env should stay local and should not be committed to GitHub.

Backend

The backend is built with Python and FastAPI.

It is responsible for:

Receiving restaurant search requests from the frontend

Accepting:

cuisine

location

search radius in miles

result limit

Converting the location into latitude and longitude using the Geoapify Geocoding API

Converting the selected search radius from miles into meters

Searching for nearby restaurants using the Geoapify Places API

Retrieving additional restaurant details when available

Returning restaurant information to the frontend as JSON

Main Backend Files

backend/app.py

Runs the FastAPI application and provides endpoints such as:

GET /
GET /health
GET /restaurants

Example request:

/restaurants?cuisine=Chinese&location=Boston&miles=5&limit=10

backend/results_pipeline.py

Contains the restaurant search logic, including:

Geoapify API key loading

Location geocoding

Cuisine search

Nearby restaurant search

Restaurant detail retrieval

Distance handling

Result formatting

Frontend

The frontend is built with:

HTML

CSS

JavaScript

It is responsible for:

Collecting the user's search information

Sending requests to the FastAPI backend

Receiving restaurant JSON data

Creating restaurant result cards

Displaying:

restaurant name

address

distance

price when available

photo when available

Showing a styled placeholder when no restaurant image is available

Opening restaurant coordinates in OpenStreetMap

Handling loading, error, and no-results states

Adapting to desktop, tablet, and mobile screen sizes

The search interface contains:

Cuisine | Location | Miles | Results | Find Restaurants

Example:

Cuisine: Thai
Location: Boston
Miles: 10
Results: 10

Data Source

Restaurant and location information is retrieved using the Geoapify API, which uses OpenStreetMap data.

Some restaurant information may not be available in the source data, including:

Photos

Prices

Phone numbers

Websites

Opening hours

The frontend handles missing data gracefully by displaying fallback text such as:

Price unavailable

and a styled restaurant placeholder when no image is available.

Setup

1. Clone the Repository

git clone YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL
cd boston-food-finder

2. Create a Virtual Environment

On macOS or Linux:

python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate

3. Install Backend Dependencies

python3 -m pip install -r backend/requirements.txt

The backend uses packages including:

fastapi
uvicorn
requests
python-dotenv

4. Configure the Geoapify API Key

Create a .env file in the project root:

boston-food-finder/.env

Add:

GEOAPIFY_API_KEY=your_geoapify_api_key_here

Replace the placeholder with your real Geoapify API key.

Important

Do not commit the .env file.

Your .gitignore should include:

.env
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

Running the Application

The frontend and backend run separately, so use two Terminal windows.

Terminal 1 — Start the Backend

From the project root:

cd backend
uvicorn app:app --reload

The backend should start at:

http://127.0.0.1:8000

You can verify the API is running by opening:

http://127.0.0.1:8000/

Expected response:

{
  "message": "Boston Food Finder API is running"
}

Health endpoint:

http://127.0.0.1:8000/health

FastAPI documentation:

http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs

Terminal 2 — Start the Frontend

Open another Terminal window.

From the project root:

cd frontend
python3 -m http.server 5500

Open the frontend in a browser:

http://localhost:5500

Example Search

Enter:

Cuisine: Chinese
Location: Boston
Miles: 5
Results: 10

Then click:

Find Restaurants

The frontend sends a request similar to:

http://127.0.0.1:8000/restaurants?cuisine=Chinese&location=Boston&miles=5&limit=10

Application flow:

User Search
    ↓
Frontend JavaScript
    ↓
FastAPI Backend
    ↓
Geoapify Geocoding API
    ↓
Geoapify Places API
    ↓
Restaurant Results
    ↓
JSON Response
    ↓
Frontend Restaurant Cards

API Parameters

Parameter

Description

Example

cuisine

Type of food

Thai

location

Search location

Boston, MA

miles

Search radius in miles

5

limit

Maximum number of results

10

Example:

/restaurants?cuisine=Italian&location=Boston&miles=5&limit=10

The current application supports:

Maximum search radius: 50 miles
Maximum results: 20 restaurants

Troubleshooting

ModuleNotFoundError

If Python cannot find a package such as requests, fastapi, or dotenv:

source venv/bin/activate
python3 -m pip install -r backend/requirements.txt

Geoapify API Key Not Found

Make sure .env exists in the project root and contains:

GEOAPIFY_API_KEY=your_actual_api_key

Do not place the real API key in source files.

Frontend Cannot Reach Backend

Make sure both servers are running at the same time:

Backend:

http://127.0.0.1:8000

Frontend:

http://localhost:5500

CSS Changes Are Not Showing

On macOS Chrome, perform a hard refresh:

Command + Shift + R

You can also verify the stylesheet is loading by opening:

http://localhost:5500/styles.css

Future Integration

The restaurant search flow can later be connected directly to the project's chat interface.

For example, chat output could provide:

{
  "cuisine": "Thai",
  "location": "Boston",
  "miles": 5,
  "limit": 10
}

Those values can then be passed directly to the restaurant search pipeline.

Security

Never commit:

.env

Do not place API keys directly inside:

app.py
results_pipeline.py
app.js
index.html

API keys should remain in local environment variables.

